<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adalbold II of Utrecht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1026</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Adalbold of Utrecht</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adalbold II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Utrecht from 1010 to 1026 and one of the learned churchmen of the Ottonian court. A supporter of Emperor Henry II, whose life he wrote, he received counties and royal rights that laid the foundations of the bishops' secular principality. He rebuilt Utrecht cathedral, consecrated in 1023, and wrote on mathematics in correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Gerbert of Aurillac</a>.</p></div>
